<Layout title="For the recipe keeper"><section class="edit-page wrap narrow"><div class="page-heading"><span class="eyebrow">KEEP THE BOOK GROWING</span><h1>For the recipe keeper.</h1><p>Add a favourite. Change a photograph. Make this little book your own.</p></div><div class="notice"><strong>Edit your cookbook in Pages CMS.</strong> Sign in with GitHub and allow Pages CMS access to your cookbook repository. Saving a change starts the publishing workflow once GitHub Pages is enabled. Check for a green tick in GitHub Actions before refreshing the cookbook.</div><a class="button" href="https://app.pagescms.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the real Pages CMS editor ↗</a><p class="upload-hint">Sign in with GitHub, then select your cookbook repository{settings.cmsRepository?` (${settings.cmsRepository})`:''}. Only the recipe keeper needs an account.</p><div class="guide-grid"><div><h2>Your first setup.</h2><ol><li>Create a free GitHub account, if you don’t already have one.</li><li>Create a <strong>public</strong> repository named <strong>{settings.cmsRepository?.split("/").pop() || "our-little-cookbook"}</strong> and add the supplied project files. Your recipes and photos will be public.</li><li>In that repository, open <strong>Settings → Pages</strong> and choose <strong>GitHub Actions</strong> as the source.</li><li>Open <strong>Actions → Publish cookbook → Run workflow</strong>. When it finishes with a green tick, open the website address shown in Pages settings.</li><li>Open Pages CMS, sign in with GitHub and allow its GitHub App access to this repository. The editing forms are already configured.</li><li>In <strong>Site settings</strong>, paste the confirmed GitHub Pages URL into “Published cookbook address”. Use that address for the birthday QR.</li></ol></div><div><h2>Add your first recipe.</h2><ol><li>In Pages CMS, select <strong>Recipes → New</strong>.</li><li>Fill in the name, permanent address, servings and times. Leave “Published” off while you work.</li><li>Choose a photograph. Add its description for anyone using a screen reader.</li><li>Add ingredient groups. Enter quantities as numbers, and choose how they should scale.</li><li>Add the method steps and drag to rearrange them. Enter any timer in minutes.</li><li>Optionally add a timeline, memories, substitutions or cooking tips.</li><li>Turn <strong>Published</strong> on and save. Check for a green publishing run in GitHub, then refresh the public cookbook.</li></ol></div></div><details open><summary>Change the cover, message, colours or collections</summary><p>Open “Site settings” in Pages CMS. You can change the title, welcome message, signature, cover image, personal introduction photo, footer, categories and colours. Turn off “Cover is a placeholder” when you upload your own cover.

Choose recipes by name in the featured list and collections. Reorder the selections to choose their display order. Colour fields accept a six-digit hex colour, such as #4F5B3A. Keep the text dark and the background light so it remains easy to read.</p></details><details><summary>Edit a recipe without breaking its link</summary><p>Open the recipe and change its text, ingredients, photos or step order. Keep its permanent address name unchanged. If you really need to change it, add the previous address name to “Previous recipe addresses” before saving. This keeps an old recipe link working.</p></details><details><summary>Photographs and uploads</summary><p>Use JPG, PNG, WebP or AVIF. Export iPhone HEIC photographs to JPG first. A width around 1,200–1,600 pixels is plenty for most cookbook pictures; aim for less than 1 MB each. Pages CMS uploads images into the repository. Give each meaningful image a short description. Avoid uploading a private photograph you wouldn’t want anyone with the website address to see.</p></details><details><summary>Check a change has published</summary><p>In GitHub, open Actions. The newest “Publish cookbook” run should have a green tick. A red cross means the change did not publish; the previous website normally remains available. Open the failed run to see the content-validation message. Refresh the public page after the green tick. A recipe with “Published” off will stay out of the public cookbook.</p></details><details><summary>Keep a backup</summary><p>Every saved CMS change becomes a GitHub commit, so you have a history of your recipes and photographs. Once a month, choose Code → Download ZIP in GitHub and keep the file somewhere safe. Keep your original photos too. Enable two-factor authentication on your account. Keep the repository name and account username the same to preserve the printed QR address.</p></details><details><summary>Before the birthday</summary><p>Replace the welcome text with your own words and remove the demo recipes. Add at least one real recipe. Test it on your boyfriend’s kind of phone. Open the final public address without signing in, then generate and scan the QR. Do a practice cooking session so you can check the timings and wording of your own recipe.</p></details><p class="small-note">A detailed setup and maintenance guide is included with the project download. This page is a guide and a link to Pages CMS; it does not impersonate an editing dashboard.</p></section></Layout>
